#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184095 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184197 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8063bc9c-a10d-562b-93b1-508b4b26a674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Singular Values of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "fcd3c46b-a87f-5ca8-9cf5-d9444a9c8218" :question/math-academy-id "e-8298" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Any $1\\times4$ matrix $A$ has at most $1$ singular value.\n\nII. Any $4\\times2$ matrix $A$ has at most $2$ distinct singular values.\n\nIII. The matrix ![](images/b8/b8601b1e66ef1d302e7194f262f6a513063bba2114fb063c2fc16542d4684392.svg) has one negative singular value." :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ is a $1 \\times 4$ matrix and $A^T$ is a $4\\times 1$ matrix, $A^T \\! A$ must be a $4 \\times 4$ matrix: \n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 4} \\times {\\large 1}}{A^T} \\times \\underset{{\\large 1} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 4}}{A} =  \n\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 4} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 4}}{A^T \\! A}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $4$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $A$ is a $4 \\times 2$ matrix and $A^T$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix, $A^T \\! A$ must be a $2 \\times 2$ matrix: \n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2} \\times {\\large 4}}{A^T} \\times \\underset{{\\large 4} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2}}{A} = \n\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2}}{A^T \\! A}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $2$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement III is false. All singular values of any matrix are non-negative. So, $A$ cannot have a negative singular value.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184096 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2bf7196-b681-5ff6-bf16-24acefa04e6c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Singular Values of a Square Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "2b9ef509-4165-5772-81c1-9a3d78424dd1" :question/math-academy-id "e-8299" :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix ![](images/85/85f9ccea6cd39f07174651d07316218edf50041f9d373e22cdaedf0c779caaf3.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$  \n\nFirst, we calculate $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )^{2}-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184097 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e70dec8f-9828-5fe7-bdb7-47e6557fc889" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Singular Values of a Rectangular Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "3fc137a7-9f7a-58de-ac92-1c7c2bdd53f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-8300" :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix ![](images/59/590d5dc32950efa0fce63a5d64a5595210a3ccb08c613e364586dba4822d7314.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$  \n\nFirst, we calculate $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1-\\lambda  \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )^{2}-(1-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =2,\\,1,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184197 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8063bc9c-a10d-562b-93b1-508b4b26a674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Singular Values of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36eba7dc-6041-512a-861b-afc8fb49a806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495637} {:answer/id #uuid "40fdca2f-83f4-5df1-91ac-71d9cae34022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495660} {:answer/id #uuid "60f43eb3-cb35-56ea-ac3e-0a1959a7891c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495728} {:answer/id #uuid "847726dd-ce3c-59a8-b0a3-56c25e5f0e39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495795} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ede84f-1362-5752-926a-19b15b77d6de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40fdca2f-83f4-5df1-91ac-71d9cae34022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495660} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4af41fc-239f-5a7e-a80b-a2420f5b8338" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "afa39ae4-c580-535b-a23d-346f2dd8aacb" :question/math-academy-id "q-139495" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Any $4\\times 2$ matrix $A$ has at most $2$ distinct singular values.\n\nII. Any matrix of the form  $A=[\\begin{aligned}-a & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{aligned}]$ has exactly one negative and one positive singular value.\n\nIII. Any $2\\times 4$ matrix $A$ has at most $4$ distinct singular values." :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $A$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $2\\times 2$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{2\\times 4}\\times {A}_{4\\times 2}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $2$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is false.  All singular values of any matrix are non-negative. So, \n$A$  can't have any negative singular value.\n\n- Statement III is true.  Indeed,  since $A$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $4\\times 4$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{4\\times 2}\\times {A}_{2\\times 4}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{4\\times 4}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $4$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e70dec8f-9828-5fe7-bdb7-47e6557fc889" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Singular Values of a Rectangular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "547cd0d6-fd5f-5b94-99cd-d4762af1fc66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5,0,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367549} {:answer/id #uuid "427695ad-2a8a-55af-9742-265b5d33144e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25,5,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367550} {:answer/id #uuid "49754909-9c5a-50e2-bda8-1f655927811b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5,5,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367551} {:answer/id #uuid "b92ac663-8b9a-5f7f-855b-b8691c3e6c16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5},0,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367552} {:answer/id #uuid "edca49b3-568f-5e5b-91e9-bd57fa31411b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5,1,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b92ac663-8b9a-5f7f-855b-b8691c3e6c16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5},0,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367552} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d25d269-9cbc-53c8-af16-a9f6f0118125" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40fb180a-20af-5d07-ba44-09c0a1ca7e05" :question/math-academy-id "q-139512" :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda [(1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-4)] & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda ({\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +4-4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}(\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,0,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{5} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{0}=0 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416481 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184197 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8063bc9c-a10d-562b-93b1-508b4b26a674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Singular Values of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc84e201-9a3b-5b9c-b271-053b83bc1062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454190} {:answer/id #uuid "b48f37d9-b3b3-5755-8843-513b5d12ef8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc84e201-9a3b-5b9c-b271-053b83bc1062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454190} :answer-field/id #uuid "13a9bc41-dd9b-55b8-81b0-283e2528d477" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454845} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52687df0-1468-50e5-b467-bf1e68ad8627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454192} {:answer/id #uuid "47e41440-ce24-5373-9725-7970eff71e7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52687df0-1468-50e5-b467-bf1e68ad8627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454192} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff9c9bed-0a04-5ac5-9daa-956eebe2c80a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454846} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "339ff9c4-e5b0-5f71-b0a6-e74576728ce2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454194} {:answer/id #uuid "57d27c03-79c7-5bcb-8497-d684476d3e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57d27c03-79c7-5bcb-8497-d684476d3e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454195} :answer-field/id #uuid "179dae7d-b2f1-55f0-bd24-81b68af5b4fa" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5650e0d2-585c-5516-892d-5eacef061cb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-95266" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Any $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $2$ distinct singular values.\n- **II.** The matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ has one negative singular value.\n- **III.** Any $5\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values." :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 2}\\times {A}_{2\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is false. All singular values of any matrix are non-negative. So, $A$ can't have a negative singular value.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 5$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 5}\\times {A}_{5\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e70dec8f-9828-5fe7-bdb7-47e6557fc889" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Singular Values of a Rectangular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45ae9be6-e3e6-59d4-b281-a63c94591f2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385847} {:answer/id #uuid "40049e3f-bffa-5f0e-a9c8-2bba4686c07e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385848} {:answer/id #uuid "085d1d56-b1dd-5cbd-a4fb-cac8147c91c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385849} {:answer/id #uuid "96138058-3bf5-5f68-b326-4f584f2e0377" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385850} {:answer/id #uuid "7a00dcda-5aaf-5a42-9073-65e57806a12a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12$ and $9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40049e3f-bffa-5f0e-a9c8-2bba4686c07e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385848} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd61e26a-f154-5f32-82ad-e5ead117f18e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ea7fe9d-28ed-5f54-bf4a-f9c31ca64b5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-95307" :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,1 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ \\,\\,-1 & 0\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,1 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ \\,\\,-1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,1 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ \\,\\,-1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,1 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ \\,\\,-1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416482 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2bf7196-b681-5ff6-bf16-24acefa04e6c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Singular Values of a Square Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c448a4f-2853-5a0a-adb6-34c3fe973ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416441} {:answer/id #uuid "392bfe36-8b63-5aac-ad95-fe5e63ae7b56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416442} {:answer/id #uuid "4f699d44-55da-5c4f-a3d1-373644f33ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416449} {:answer/id #uuid "514af78b-310e-50a2-b1ef-8f945c043cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$16$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416450} {:answer/id #uuid "b85ee86c-0aac-52bc-b5db-5824de7a6bdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$25$ and $9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f699d44-55da-5c4f-a3d1-373644f33ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416449} :answer-field/id #uuid "186b2a25-d332-5f01-a6a2-293d5802ee3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97ca7c6b-3d02-595e-9b24-0e63b350342b" :question/math-academy-id "q-95367" :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 17\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}17-\\lambda  & -8 \\\\ -8 & 17-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (17-\\lambda )(17-\\lambda )-64 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-34\\lambda +289-64 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-34\\lambda +225 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -25) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =25,\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{25}=5 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{9}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385852 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2bf7196-b681-5ff6-bf16-24acefa04e6c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Singular Values of a Square Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57ba2282-d4ab-5fbf-9130-d06a2eed65a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3,\\,2,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385854} {:answer/id #uuid "b45ecc1a-07a7-50a1-a446-cf4a96064ceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2,\\,2,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385855} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a83de7-3a30-52e2-a29e-09bdd3418bd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5,\\,2,\\,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385856} {:answer/id #uuid "01e6f9da-3643-528a-9879-9df01fb0203d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9,\\,4,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385857} {:answer/id #uuid "d58280f6-ca95-52bf-80d9-6a8986b3c8bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4,\\,1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57ba2282-d4ab-5fbf-9130-d06a2eed65a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3,\\,2,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385854} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a01643e-564d-5068-bf87-5773a11495bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "63c30a7c-7d2d-5a6c-adcf-5b929543dd1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-95369" :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 8-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 8-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )((5-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +36) & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda (\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,4,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
